# B25S: selected JSON migration preparation service

This shared Rust service supports B25a/B25b/B25c without duplicating native helper behavior. It preserves the native first namespace entry (including last values at original duplicate-key positions), local EClass subtype predicates, and selection of object-valued children of matching typed parents. Selection is collected per pass before removal; passes run in supplied order. Object preorder IDs and postorder application preserve final tree effects without copying ancestor key strings.

Prepared.json is an inspection Value. Composition must use Prepared.to_json(), which writes current namespace values in retained insertion order. Canonical Value serialization can change participant eligibility. Tests cover matching/foreign-first resources, duplicate aliases, removed first aliases and replay. Input/output admission is bounded at1MiB,depth64 and65536JSONvalues. Serialization rechecks edited values and streams into a bounded writer. These are selected candidate limits, not native capacity qualification.

The output carries a selected participant version but never persists it or rewrites native global migration history. The caller owns whole-project atomic publication. No SQL, network, resource loading, application host or Java delegate occurs in production. Native Java sources below are development-only oracles.

Coordinator support-04 passes nine Rust regressions, formatting, all-target Clippy and release probe build. Earlier support-01/support-02/support-03 captures remain preserved and attributed to their original source. Static reviewers found and corrected path-key amplification and namespace-composition risks before acceptance; no failure is relabeled.

Forty-four raw input fixtures match actual Java participant observations for output values, version, resource predicate and success/refusal. Java compiles the exact pinned four participant/helper source files with annotation processing disabled, against nine identified runtime libraries from official2026.9.3. All728 four-parent subtype predicates across182domainclassifiers match. Four fresh version-ordered composition cases also match. This is selected source-compiled runtime evidence; it does not bind the whole release artifact to the target source or qualify migration-framework lifecycle, native deserialization, PostgreSQL or application UI. Native exception classes/messages are retained; Rust exposes typed errors rather than asserting Java exception text equality.

The fixed long-key release probe uses143494inputbytes, a128KiB ancestor key and256matchingchildren, under512MiB address-space caps. Actual-process peak RSS is36480KiB before and3264KiB at final support-04,91.1percent lower for this workload. Compilation is excluded. Earlier post-fix3456KiB capture remains separate. Timing fields are single observations, not median/p95 or general performance guarantees. Final binary is retained externally; the pre-fix binary was hash-recorded but not separately retained before the shared target was overwritten. Its original source, lock and build command remain archived.

Original native/probe outputs, participant sources and exact sequence runner are retained with hashes. The initial Rust observation-probe build used an inline coordinator recipe; its original wrapper/build streams were not separately archived. The compiled probe source, binary, observation argv and raw outputs are retained; the later four-case sequence has a full pre-execution runner archive and unchanged-input receipt. No broader evidence claim is made.

B25a–c will target this prerequisite branch after source composition. Land prerequisites through review, retarget children, inspect and revalidate actual integration. Existing held model/factory/initializer paths remain untouched.
